If you want to make a GameObject in Unity3d not to respond to TouchEvent, you can make use of the raycasting function and the layers feature in Unity. Basically, by putting game objects in different layers, you can make certain game objects not to respond to touch events.

Here are the general steps:

1. Arrange your GameObjects in layers
   Go to your GameObject, in the Inspector window, at the top-right corner, you will see Layer - Default. Click it and select "Add Layer".

2. Add a new Layer, name it "NoTouch"

3. Go back to your GameObject, and now assign it to the new Layer "NoTouch".

4. Adjust your Touch code
   On the script handling your touch input, use Raycast and ensure it doesn't touch objects in the "NoTouch" layer:

```csharp
void Update()
{
    if (Input.touchCount > 0 && Input.GetTouch(0).phase == TouchPhase.Began)
    {
        Ray ray = Camera.main.ScreenPointToRay(Input.GetTouch(0).position);
        RaycastHit hit;

        // Here LayerMask 9 is the "NoTouch" layer. Change it to fit your need.
        if (!Physics.Raycast(ray, out hit, Mathf.Infinity, 1<<9))
        {
             // Handle your touch event, it's not hit anything on "NoTouch" layer.
             print("Touched " + hit.transform.gameObject.name);
        }
    }
}
```
With this setting, all objects in the "NoTouch" layer won't respond to touch events because we're specifically excluding that layer in the Raycast check.

